
Databases allow programs to store data permanently so it can be retrieved, changed and searched later.

Python variables disappear when a program stops. A database lets your program persist data.

A typical application might store:
- Users
- Products
- Orders
- Messages
- Posts
- Payments
- Scores
- Settings

The two major areas to understand are:
- SQL -> the language used to work with relational databases
- Python database tools -> how Python programs communicate with databases

---

## Relational Databases

A relational database stores information in tables.

Example:

USERS

| id | name | email |
|---|---|---|
| 1 | Ben | ben@example.com |
| 2 | Alice | alice@example.com |
| 3 | Tom | tom@example.com |

ORDERS

| id | user_id | product | price |
|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | 1 | Laptop | 1000 |
| 2 | 1 | Mouse | 30 |
| 3 | 2 | Keyboard | 80 |

The tables can be related using keys.

---

## Tables

A table stores one type of entity.

Examples:
- users
- products
- orders
- messages
- courses

Each table contains:
- Columns -> describe the data
- Rows -> individual records

Example:

```sql
CREATE TABLE users (
    id INTEGER,
    name TEXT,
    email TEXT
);